Abstract Data Types (ADT)
--List Example (Used a lot)
--Array Lists
--Linked Lists

ADT: Doesn't necessarily have a structure associated with it. List of operations
that we want to be able to perform.
Very much like an Interface.

List: Elements have some sort of order to them.
--get(i)
--set(i)
--add(i)
--remove(i)

WEEK 3 - DAY 2
Iterator's job is to move through a collection...Forward.
--Has 3 methods
---Boolean - hasNext();
---next(); // Does 2 things
----What location are we at?
----Moves to the next location.
---remove(); // Removes element.

List Interface extends Collection
-Any method in Collection also exists in List.

ListIterator
--Can Move forward AND backwards.

Big-O Costs:
Insert has a cost greater than O(1); it is O(N)

-ArrayList
--ADVANTAGE: Calls to Get or Set take constantant time, so they're very fast
--DISADVANTAGE: Calls to Add or Remove can be expensive
--Add items to the back is an O(C) cost. In a forloop it is O(N)
--Add Items to the front is an O(N) cost. In a forloop it is O(N^2)
--Compute the sum in a list is an O(C) cost. In a forloop it is O(N)

-LinkedList
--ADVANTAGE: Calls to Add or Remove are cheap provided that the position is known. So much so, that
methods already exist for addFirst, removeFirst, addLast, removeLast, getFirst, and getLast.
--DISADVANTAGE: The list is not easily indexable, so regular calls to get and set can be expensive.
--Add items to the back is an O(C) cost. In a forloop it is O(N)
--Add Items to the front is an O(C) cost. In a forloop it is O(N)
--Compute the sum in a list is an O(N) cost. In a forloop it is O(N^2)...use enhanced forloop instead.

Programming by contract: This is what the user guarantees they would give. What it knows it could return. etc

Stack
- The idea is that you would add and remove elements from one end of the list.
--Static vs Non-Static nested class
--Non-Static: Tied to the particular instance

